{
  "case_id": "2016-16-369",
  "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:21 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Your Honor, when a police officer reasonably thinks to himself, as Deputy Conley did here, this is where I'm going to die, he has to be free to make the split-second decision to defend himself and those around him. Any legal rule that says that is unreasonable is untenable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And so the question to me is not that one. The question is when does the police officer pay the victim who is suffering for that loss if the victim had nothing to do with causing the loss?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioners",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the answer to that question is quite likely the officer, but not because of the provocation rule, but rather because the officer has committed a clearly unconstitutional act, which is not just entering, but also failing to knock and announce, clear violation of clearly established law, which then proximately causes what -- what unfurls next."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can I ask about the first theory, because you said if there was a violation -- excuse me -- of the knock-and-announce rule, and a death or an injury proximately caused by that violation, then there could be recovery. Is that what you said?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "So I guess the question is, why is this case any different? In other words, this case you don't have the knock-and-announce violation; you have a different constitutional violation, which is an unauthorized entry. And why would it not be the case that the -- that the death or injury in such a case is also proximately caused by that violation?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0006",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "You said something about knock and announce. I say why isn't the same theory applicable when the constitutional violation is unauthorized entry into somebody's home?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0007",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "injury that results when somebody barges into, goes into somebody's home without legal authorization, that one of the things that foreseeably can result from that is a shooting?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0009",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 11,
        "start": 11
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Can -- can you tell me if Paula Jones on your theory -- Paula Hughes -- Paula Hughes, the homeowner, who was -- there was no warrant and she was placed in a car and treated rather badly, would she have a 1983 claim?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0010",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It might very well change the outcome. When somebody shows up with a warrant, they show it -- they show the warrant, they have legal authority, people act very differently than when somebody shows up with no warrant and says, I don't have authority. I'm coming in anyway."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0011",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 14,
        "start": 14
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Why -- why do you break -- why is everyone breaking it down in this way?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0013",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "question, which I think goes to the first question presented. And I think the question was, why can't we just say that we can consider the facts of causing the situation as part of the reasonableness inquiry? And the answer there is because the question that we're answering under Graham is, was the officer's force -- use of force reasonable in the circumstances that he faced?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0000-0015",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 19,
        "start": 19
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "by doing their jobs, don't -- aren't responsible for creating the dangerous situation and don't forfeit the ability to defend themselves --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. Saharsky (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. SAHARSKY: Well, I think you would ask in that question what -- whether there really was a threat that the officer was responding to, and I think you're positing a situation where there really wasn't a threat there. But I think in this circumstance, everyone recognized that there was a threat facing these officers who are going about doing their police business in good faith, and they see a person pointing what appeared to be a gun at them."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0001-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "ORAL ARGUMENT OF LEONARD J. FELDMAN ON BEHALF OF THE RESPONDENTS"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: But -- but then why are we talking about the failure to get a search warrant? Why -- why did that make a difference?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FELDMAN: The conduct is what we use to define proximate cause."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kagan",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KAGAN: Now the question is, is it a but -- is it a proximate cause of the inquiry?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. FELDMAN: Well, the proximate cause comes from the two things that the officers did that led directly and foreseeably to the confrontation and the violent confrontation with the homeowners. One is that they decided to enter, and that was unconstitutional. And the other is that they went in without giving any notice."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Counsel",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0003-0000",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "If there are no further questions, we respectfully request that the Court reverse the Ninth Circuit's judgment."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "54bca404abd9a1c55dab4b6233cbe8b24c4d339874e08b5db7ed70a1c89199e3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-54bca404abd9-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Thank you, counsel. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2016-16-369",
      "confidence": 0.0,
